Matt Levine's Money Stuff column "Attention is all you need" [](cite:levine2026) argues that agentic artificial intelligence will reshape retail-facing financial businesses by changing how much attention customers pay to their finances. This writeup follows that argument and supplies the financial background the column assumes.

Most of that background is explained elsewhere. The banks' side of the argument rests on the [deposit spread](../../concepts/net-interest-margin/#the-deposit-spread) behind a bank's [net interest margin](../../concepts/net-interest-margin/). The brokerages' side rests on [market making](../../concepts/adverse-selection/#market-making), on the [adverse selection](../../concepts/adverse-selection/#adverse-selection) that makes retail orders valuable to a market maker, and on the [payment for order flow](../../concepts/adverse-selection/#payment-for-order-flow) that results, together with the margins of [prediction markets and sportsbooks](../../concepts/prediction-markets/).

# Dispersion trade

A dispersion trade bets that outcomes within a group of related assets will diverge, some doing well while others do badly. It takes no view on the direction of the group as a whole, and it profits when its predictions about relative performance are right, whether the group goes up or down.

The literal version of the trade lives in derivatives markets. A trader who expects the technology stocks of the S&P 500 to move little as a sector, but to swing widely in different directions one by one as some report blowout earnings and others miss expectations, sells options on an index of the sector and buys options on the individual stocks. If the stocks move sharply but in offsetting directions, the index stays roughly flat and the options sold on it expire harmlessly, while the options on the individual stocks gain value from the large moves. The trader profits from the _dispersion_ of returns across the group.

What the trade is really exposed to is correlation. An index that holds its stocks with weights $w_i$, where stock $i$ has volatility $\sigma_i$ and the returns of stocks $i$ and $j$ have correlation $\rho_{ij}$ (with $\rho_{ii} = 1$), has a volatility $\sigma_I$ given by

$$
	\sigma_I^2 = \sum_{i} \sum_{j} w_i w_j \rho_{ij} \sigma_i \sigma_j. @tag(index-variance)
$$

In the simplest case of $n$ equally weighted stocks, each with the same volatility $\sigma$ and every pair with the same correlation $\rho$, [](eq:index-variance) reduces to

$$
	\sigma_I = \sigma \sqrt{\frac{1}{n} + \Big(1 - \frac{1}{n}\Big)\rho}.
$$

<IndexVolatility />

When the stocks all move together, with $\rho$ near one, the index is as volatile as its members, and options on the members gain nothing that options on the index do not. When they move independently of one another, their moves largely cancel inside the index: twenty stocks with a volatility of 30% each and a pairwise correlation of $0.2$ make an index with a volatility of only about 15%. The prices of index options and of single-stock options together imply a level of correlation, and a dispersion trade is, at its core, a bet that correlation will turn out lower than that.

The term is also used more loosely, for any investment thesis that picks winners and losers within a single theme. A rise in interest rates, for example, might be good for banks that earn large [net interest margins](../../concepts/net-interest-margin/) but bad for mortgage lenders whose refinancing volume dries up, and an investor with that view would buy bank stocks and sell mortgage lender stocks. In either sense the trade is _market-neutral_ with respect to its theme: the sector need not go up or down, and only the predictions about which participants win and which lose must be correct. The risk is that the winners and losers are misidentified, or that an unexpected shock moves the entire group together and overwhelms the relative positions.

# The wheel strategy

The wheel is a popular options strategy that generates regular income from option premiums by cycling mechanically between selling put options and selling call options on a single stock. It is built from the two basic kinds of [option](../../concepts/european-option/): a put, which gives its buyer the right to sell a stock at a fixed strike price by an expiration date, and a call, which gives the right to buy. The seller of either, its writer, collects a premium in cash at the outset for taking on the obligation, and each contract covers 100 shares.

**Step 1: sell a cash-secured put.** The trader chooses a stock that they would be willing to own and writes a put on it, setting aside enough cash to buy the stock if the put is exercised, which is what makes the put _cash-secured_. Suppose shares of Acme Corp are trading at \$100, and the trader sells one put with a strike price of \$95 and an expiration one month away for a premium of \$2.00 per share, or \$200, setting aside \$9,500. If Acme stays above \$95 through expiration, the put expires worthless: the trader keeps the \$200 and repeats Step 1. If Acme falls to \$93, the put is exercised: the trader must buy 100 shares at \$95 each and now owns stock worth \$9,300, an unrealized loss of \$200 that the premium offsets. The trader moves to Step 2.

**Step 2: sell a covered call.** The trader writes a call on the shares now held, a _covered_ call because the shares can be delivered if it is exercised. With Acme at \$93, the trader sells one call with a strike price of \$97 and an expiration one month away for a premium of \$1.50 per share, or \$150. If Acme stays below \$97 through expiration, the call expires worthless: the trader keeps the \$150, still owns the stock, and repeats Step 2. If Acme rises to \$99, the call is exercised: the trader sells the 100 shares at \$97, \$2 above their cost, keeps the premium along with the \$200 gain on the shares, and returns to Step 1.

<WheelPayoff />

Over the two months of the example the wheel collected \$350 in premiums and a \$200 capital gain. The figure shows what that income is paid for. In both steps the trader's profit is capped above the strike and falls dollar for dollar with the stock below it, so the main risk is a significant decline in the stock: if Acme falls to \$70 while the trader is holding it, the loss of \$25 per share, \$2,500 on 100 shares, far exceeds the few hundred dollars of premium collected. The strategy also underperforms in strong bull markets. If Acme rises from \$100 to \$150, the trader would have been better off simply holding the stock, because the covered call capped the gain at the strike price. It works best on stable or slowly rising stocks.

The wheel is frequently discussed in the context of automated trading because it is entirely rules-based. Which strike to choose, which expiration, and when to sell the next option all follow a mechanical process that a system can carry out continuously, without human judgment on each trade and whether or not the customer is logged in.

# A dispersion trade on attention

The author draws on two Wall Street Journal articles published on the same day. One covers how artificial intelligence threatens the "laziness tax" that banks earn from inattentive customers. The other covers Robinhood's annual event, where the company unveiled agent features including an in-app assistant that can research investments, build strategies, and eventually place trades autonomously through a feature called "Loops", which runs strategies repeatedly even when the user is not logged in.

The author frames the argument as a dispersion trade in the looser sense: no derivatives position is described, and the concept serves as a mental model for how agents will create winners and losers within the financial sector. Banks, insurers, and mortgage companies profit when customers pay _too little_ attention. They are built on customer inertia, and an agent that tirelessly optimizes a customer's finances threatens their revenue. Retail brokerages, sportsbooks, and prediction markets profit when customers pay _more_ attention, or at least generate more activity, and they benefit when agents trade more frequently on customers' behalf. The author's trade is to be short the first group and long the second. The framing is what makes the argument more interesting than the observation that artificial intelligence will change finance: within a single theme, retail finance, the same technology has opposite effects according to which side of the attention trade a business sits on.

# Businesses that profit from inattention

See also: [Net interest margin](../../concepts/net-interest-margin/)

A bank lends or invests the money deposited with it at market rates and pays its depositors less, and the difference, its [deposit spread](../../concepts/net-interest-margin/#the-deposit-spread), is one of the largest sources of profit in traditional banking: a savings account might pay 0.5% while the bank earns 4% or 5% on the money. The spread survives because most customers do not shop around for the best savings rate or move money out of a low-yield account, so that when market rates rise, deposit rates do not rise to match, a lag measured by the [deposit beta](../../concepts/net-interest-margin/#deposit-beta). The author calls this a "laziness tax" and notes that Americans forgo "untold sums" of money every year because of it.

An agent acting on a customer's behalf would have no such inertia. It would continuously compare the rate a bank pays against competitors and market alternatives and move money to whichever account offers the best return, so that the bank must either pay depositors closer to market rates or watch deposits leave. The author refers to "agentic bank runs", in which agents collectively pull deposits out of banks that offer below-market rates, potentially destabilizing banks that rely on sticky deposits for funding. In the author's phrase, an agent will "extract the last basis point from banks". A [basis point](../../concepts/net-interest-margin/#basis-points) is one hundredth of a percentage point, worth \$1 a year on \$10,000 of savings, and the point is that an agent has effectively unlimited attention, so it will pursue gains too small for a person to bother with.

The same logic applies to other products built on inertia. Life insurance policies, annuities, and mortgages are all priced with assumptions about how many customers will shop around, refinance, or switch providers, and the businesses that sell them lose revenue if agents remove that friction. The author also refers to a 2019 law review article by Rory Van Loo, "Digital Market Perfection", which explored how digital tools and algorithms could help consumers overcome information asymmetries and inertia in financial markets: essentially the same thesis, applied to an earlier generation of technology.

# Businesses that profit from activity

See also: [Market making and adverse selection](../../concepts/adverse-selection/), [Prediction markets and sportsbooks](../../concepts/prediction-markets/)

The other leg of the trade turns on how retail brokerages in the United States make money, which the author only sketches. A trade placed on an application like Robinhood does not go to a stock exchange. The brokerage routes it to a [market maker](../../concepts/adverse-selection/#market-making) such as Citadel Securities or Virtu Financial, which earns the spread between the prices at which it buys and sells and pays the brokerage for the orders, a fee called [payment for order flow](../../concepts/adverse-selection/#payment-for-order-flow). It pays for retail orders in particular because of [adverse selection](../../concepts/adverse-selection/#adverse-selection), the risk of trading against someone who knows more. A hedge fund that has researched a company in depth is a dangerous counterparty and a retail customer trading on sentiment, headlines, or personal need is a safe one, so [retail order flow](../../concepts/adverse-selection/#retail-and-institutional-order-flow) is the most profitable kind a market maker can receive. The author illustrates this with a line from an earlier column: "for equity market makers, there are few more beautiful phrases in the English language than 'day trader and volleyball-programs coordinator.'" The ideal customer trades actively, generating many spreads, and has no informational edge.

The column emphasizes that the _type_ of trading matters as well as its volume. Options, cryptocurrencies, and prediction-market contracts are especially lucrative because their [spreads are wider](../../concepts/adverse-selection/#spreads-across-instruments) than those of plain stocks. The same holds for the "agentic sports gambling" to which the column briefly refers: a sportsbook builds a margin, the [vigorish](../../concepts/prediction-markets/#sportsbook-odds-and-the-vigorish), into every bet, so an agent that places many bets generates a great deal of revenue for the house without meaningfully improving the customer's odds of winning. An agent that trades these instruments continuously, while the customer sleeps, works, or is otherwise disengaged, is therefore ideal for the brokerage and the market maker. It generates high volume, in high-margin products, without any informational edge. The author describes "enabling investors to trade … options, bitcoin and prediction-market contracts even when they are not paying attention" as "the Holy Grail of retail finance", and mentions the wheel strategy as an example of what an agent might automate on Robinhood: a mechanical cycle that produces consistent volume in options.

Brokerages are nonetheless a mixed case. Their second main revenue stream is [cash balance revenue](../../concepts/net-interest-margin/#brokerage-cash-balance-revenue), the deposit spread of a bank under another name: the brokerage invests the cash that sits uninvested in customer accounts and pays the customers a below-market rate on it, often close to zero. Unlike payment for order flow it moves _against_ attention, since an attentive customer, or an agent, would move idle cash to a higher-yielding account. On net, the author appears to think that the revenue from order flow dominates, which would explain why brokerages are embracing agents enthusiastically.
